      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Background of Indian National Movement"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Indian National Congress (INC) was founded in 1885 by A.O. Hume, with W.C. Bonnerjee serving as its first president in Bombay.",
        "The Moderate Phase (1885–1905) relied on constitutional methods, petitions, and prayers, led by leaders like Dadabhai Naoroji and G.K. Gokhale.",
        "The Extremist Phase (1905–1919) emerged post-Partition of Bengal, led by Lal-Bal-Pal, emphasizing Swadeshi, boycott, and passive resistance.",
        "The Home Rule Movement (1916) was spearheaded by Bal Gangadhar Tilak and Annie Besant to demand self-government for India.",
        "The Gandhian Phase transformed the struggle into a mass movement through Non-Cooperation (1920), Civil Disobedience (1930), and Quit India (1942)."
      ],
      "id": "v8ixlys"
    },
